#hypothesis(title: [Fibromyalgia Autoimmunity: An Antibody Subset Within a Non-HLA-Linked Architecture])[
*Certainty: 0.40.* The Kerrebijn 2026 GWAS found no significant MHC signal and no immune/glial heritability enrichment in fibromyalgia, providing genetic evidence against a primarily autoimmune (HLA-linked) architecture @Kerrebijn2026FibromyalgiaGWAS. Yet independent passive-transfer evidence demonstrates that IgG from fibromyalgia and long-COVID patients can reproduce symptomatology in mice, consistent with pathogenic autoantibodies (Origin: brainstorm; corpus: passive-IgG-transfer evidence in ch08 @Goebel2021passiveTransferFM). 

*Reconciliation.* These are not contradictory: genetic architecture (germline HLA/MHC risk) and acquired humoral autoimmunity (antibodies arising post-infection) are different layers. Fibromyalgia could carry a *non-HLA-linked* genetic vulnerability to central sensitization (the GWAS finding) while a subset of patients develops *acquired* pathogenic autoantibodies (the passive-IgG finding) as a downstream or amplifying mechanism — the antibodies need not be genetically HLA-predisposed. This mirrors the ME/CFS situation, where DecodeME found no autoimmune genetic correlation yet passive-IgG and GPCR-autoantibody evidence exists.

*Falsifiable predictions:* (1) Fibromyalgia patients who respond to immunomodulation (IVIG/plasmapheresis) will cluster in an autoantibody-positive subset that shows no enrichment for the 26 GWAS loci. (2) Passive-transfer-positive fibromyalgia sera will correlate with autoantibody titers rather than with GWAS risk-score. Falsified if the GWAS loci and the autoantibody subset are the same patients (fully overlapping), which would argue for a unified HLA-linked mechanism.

*Consequence:* This reframes the autoimmune debate: rather than "fibromyalgia is/isn't autoimmune," it proposes a two-layer model (central-sensitization genetics + acquired antibody subset) that could explain why some patients respond to immunomodulation and others do not.
] <hyp:fibromyalgia-autoimmune-reconciliation>
